Progress Quest Remix - Web
==========================

This is the in-browser edition of Progress Quest, ported from Delphi
to JavaScript, remixed.

- Progress Quest Remix site:  https://enderandrew.com/ProgressQuest/
- This fork:                  https://github.com/enderandrew/ProgressQuest
- Original web version:       https://bitbucket.org/grumdrig/pq-web
- Original game:              https://bitbucket.org/grumdrig/pq


Running it
----------

Open roster.html through a web server (the game clock runs in a Web
Worker, which browsers won't start from a file:// URL). For example:

    python3 -m http.server
    # then visit http://localhost:8000/roster.html

jQuery 4 is bundled as jquery-4.0.0.min.js, so no network is needed.


Simulator
---------

sim.js runs the real game scripts headlessly in Node, finishing every
task instantly, so you can watch a character grow to level 50 in a few
seconds. Runs with the same seed are identical.

    node sim.js --levels 50 --seed pq
    node sim.js --race "4chan Troll" --class "Barbarian Pretzel" --quiet
    node sim.js --help

Use it to check that a change behaves the way you expect before playing.


Save format
-----------

Saves carry a saveVersion number (SaveVersion in config.js). When a
change needs existing saves patched, bump SaveVersion and add a
function to SaveMigrations; old saves are upgraded once when they load.


Attribute profiles
------------------

Every race and class names a primary and a secondary attribute
("4chan Troll|STR,INT"). Together they give each character a profile:
the primary counts 2, the secondary 1, and an attribute named by both
race and class counts twice. On the character sheet, primaries get a
filled star and secondaries an open one.

- Profile attributes are more likely to go up when a stat is won
  (K.StatPickBase in config.js controls how much more).
- HP Max and MP Max in the profile grow 10% faster per point of weight
  on level-up (K.PoolGrowthPerWeight).
- The martial/arcane split (STR, CON, DEX, HP Max vs INT, WIS, CHA,
  MP Max) is shown under the stats; later it decides how often a
  character casts spells.


Combat
------

Every monster on the Killing Fields is now actually fought. combat.js
holds the rules as a pure function, ResolveCombat(hero, foe, seed): the
same character, monster and seed always give the same fight, so a
server can one day replay a character's history to catch cheaters.

The fight is settled when the task starts; the task bar plays it out,
and longer fights take longer to watch. Outcomes:

- win / close win: XP, quest and plot progress, and loot as before
- flee (you ran, or you both lost interest): a quarter of the XP
- defeat: a quarter of the XP, and the consequences below

What each stat does in a fight:

  STR     melee damage
  DEX     hit chance, dodging, acting first, getting away
  CON     softens physical hits, faster HP regen and resting
  INT     spell damage
  WIS     spell success, dodging and softening monster magic, MP regen
  CHA     beaten monsters may give up and run off
  HP Max  health, which now carries over from fight to fight
  MP Max  mana for spells; arcane characters cast more often

Monsters are as strong as a typical character of their level
(ExpectedStat and ExpectedPool, fitted to simulated characters).
K.MonMods adjectives ("+4 Giant", "-2 Fairly Stupid") make a quarter of
monsters stronger or weaker, by at most a third of their level. Gear
counts too: the weapon's and armor's power against the monster's level.

Tuning knobs are in K.Combat at the top of combat.js. sim.js prints a
fight summary; win rates measured with it (16 characters to level 30):

  monster level vs yours   -5    -2    0    +2    +5    +8
  won                      99%  98%  94%   85%   63%   22%


Spells
------

Each spell in K.Spells (config.js) is "Name|type", and the type decides
what it does in a fight. The spell book tags each spell with its type.

  damage  hurts the monster; INT makes it hit harder
  heal    restores HP; WIS makes it heal more
  buff    for the rest of the fight you hit harder and take less damage
  debuff  the monster hits softer, misses more and takes more damage
  cc      crowd control: if it lands (CHA vs the monster's WIS) the
          monster loses 2+ turns

Every cast needs WIS to succeed and costs MP. The roman-numeral level
matters: each level adds 10% to the effect but only 5% to the cost.
How often a character casts depends on its profile (15% of turns for
pure martial, 60% for pure arcane). When it does, it heals if badly
hurt, buffs and debuffs once early in the fight, sometimes tries crowd
control on a healthy monster, and otherwise casts a damage spell.
Knowing a heal spell also halves the time spent resting.

To change what a spell does, edit its type. A spell with no type (or a
misspelled one) gets a random type, always the same for the same name.


Loot
----

Gear has a power, stored as a number (hover over a slot to see it):
roughly the level it was made for. The weapon's power against the
monster's level sets your damage; the average power of the shield and
armor slots sets how much damage you shrug off.

- New gear always goes to your weakest slot, and is only equipped if it
  is better. Spare gear from quests goes in your pack to sell.
- The shop's stock is around your level, a bit better with CHA. If you
  have gold to spare it shows you premium stock, up to 5 levels better,
  priced on the same curve. You only pay for an actual upgrade.
- A beaten monster may drop its item (the chance rises with its level
  and your CHA), and some drops are rare ("Glowing orc ear of Destiny")
  and sell for much more. Monsters may also carry some gold.
- Everything you pick up, gold included, is unbanked until you sell it
  at market; the line under the inventory shows how much you carry.
- CHA also raises the prices you get when selling.

Tuning knobs are in K.Loot in combat.js.


Defeat
------

Lose a fight and:

- the monster goes through your pockets: 30-60% of your unbanked gold
  and about a third of your unsold loot. Banked gold and the gear you
  are wearing are never taken.
- someone drags you back to town ("You are dragged back to town by a
  Demi-Canadian Sailor Rune"), and you convalesce at a temple. The stay
  is longer at higher levels and shorter with more CON.
- the temple takes a tithe of 10% of your banked gold. That is small
  change early on but a real dent once you are rich, and it is the main
  brake on the late-game gold pile; it can never leave you broke.
- you are Wounded for the next 2 fights (a little weaker, a little
  softer), shown in red under the health bars along with your total
  number of defeats.
- you get cautious: each defeat makes you pick on monsters 2 levels
  weaker, and each win wears a little of that off. This is what stops a
  weak character from losing, getting robbed, losing again forever.

Tuning knobs are in K.Defeat in combat.js.


Watching
--------

The line under the task bar shows the latest blow while a fight plays
out, then how it ended ("Last fight: Barely beat the Giant Orc in 7
rounds (cost 41 HP)"). Press C, or click "Combat log", for a panel that
plays the whole fight out blow by blow as the task bar fills. The
window is up to 1200px wide and stacks into one column on narrow
screens.


Balance
-------

Checked by simulating one character for every race x class combination
(576) to level 20. Every combination wins at least 73% of its fights;
races and classes are each within 4 percentage points of one another;
martial, arcane and mixed characters within half a point.


Story
-----

Every Act has a story, picked when the Act begins and shown in a box
under Plot Development ("Act II: The Heist - {guy} hires you to steal
the {item} from the vaults of {kingdom}. It is definitely not a setup.").
When the Act's bar fills, that same story's ending plays out as the
cinematic, with the same people and places, and the next Act begins
with a new story. Hover over an Act in the list to see what it was
about and, once it is over, how it ended (handy, since the game idles
and the cinematic is easy to miss). The Prologue is always the Old Bastard(TM) taunting you in your
dreams, with a freshly generated Shakespearean insult.

Quests come from a much longer list (hunt a monster, placate it,
unionize it, escort someone, return an overdue library book...), and
about a third come from the current Act's story ("Listen to an episode
of {nemesis}'s podcast").

All of it lives in story.js: K.Stories (setup, ending and quests for
each story), K.Quests, and K.Insults. Placeholders are listed at the
top of the file. Add a story or a quest by adding an entry.


Narration
---------

The important things are read aloud with the browser's speech
synthesis: the Prologue, each new Act's story, the cinematic that ends
an Act, and random events. Fights, loot and errands are not. Press N or
click "Narration" under the task bar to turn it off or on (remembered
per browser). Browsers won't speak until you have clicked or pressed a
key on the page; until then the link says "click to enable", and the
first few held lines are read once you do.


Random events
-------------

Now and then something happens: while you rest, on the road to the
Killing Fields(TM), in town, or after a fight. Events are narrated, and
most give a small reward or cost (gold, an item, +1 to a stat, a spell,
a piece of gear, healing, a few fights Wounded, a little XP).

They live in events.js. Each one is an entry in K.Events with where it
can happen, a weight, its lines and its effect; the format is described
at the top of the file, and story placeholders ({guy}, {kingdom},
{nemesis}...) work in the lines. K.EventChance and K.EventCooldown set
how often they happen (about one every 10 minutes of play by default).
